//! Repro: yo-self ignores the `Acyclic` marker trait when deciding
//! `needs_cycle_gc`, so a program TS compiles with the LIGHTWEIGHT RC runtime
//! gets the full cycle-GC runtime instead.
//!
//! Measure on the emitted C (`--emit-c --skip-c-compiler`):
//!   TS      -> grep -c __yo_gc_collecting = 0   (lightweight, type_id dispatch)
//!   yo-self -> grep -c __yo_gc_collecting = 9   (full Bacon-Rajan cycle GC)
//!
//! ROOT CAUSE (probe-confirmed; an earlier `Acyclic`-trait hypothesis was
//! WRONG — yo-self's pre-scan already has the `!is_atomic_reference_struct_type`
//! exclusion that TS has, so Mutex is never the root):
//!
//! A probe in `compute_needs_cycle_gc` (codegen/codegen_c.yo) naming the
//! offending entry printed:
//!
//!   // PROBE cycle-root: c_name=__yo_t10 struct_root=true enum_root=false
//!   //   ty=<struct:struct_yo_id_3049>
//!
//! and `__yo_t10` is a GENERIC ArrayList whose element type erased to void:
//!
//!   struct __yo_t10_struct {   // <struct:struct_yo_id_3049>
//!     __yo_ref_header_t header;
//!     void** _ptr; size_t _length; size_t _capacity;
//!   };
//!
//! (TS monomorphizes these — `uint8_t* _ptr`, `size_t* _ptr`, … — and never
//! leaves a void** one in its type table.)
//!
//! It is treated as a CONCRETE cycle root because the pre-scan's
//! `!_struct_has_generic_field(ty)` guard says it has no generic field, and
//! that guard calls `type_contains_some_type`, which in yo-self is
//! **TOP-LEVEL ONLY** (types/utils.yo: arms for `.SomeT` and `.TypeAppT`, then
//! `_ => false`; the module header documents the simplification). The field is
//! `?*(E)` — a SomeT nested under Option/Ptr — so the top-level check misses
//! it. TS's `typeContainsSomeType` is a full structural walk and returns true.
//!
//! So the fix is to make `type_contains_some_type` recurse structurally like
//! TS. NOTE that is a HIGH-BLAST-RADIUS change: the predicate gates constructor
//! emission, specialization decisions and several codegen paths, so it needs
//! the full battery + corpus + fixpoint, and it may flip other families at the
//! same time (the ordered-collections "dispose_fn referencing a never-emitted
//! drop method" class is plausibly the same root).
//!
//!
//! DIRECTION-OF-CHANGE WARNING. Making the predicate recurse makes it return
//! TRUE more often, i.e. STRICTLY MORE CONSERVATIVE. The constructor loops skip
//! types it calls generic, so the naive fix REMOVES constructors that are
//! currently emitted and could turn green files red — the opposite of what the
//! undeclared-ctor families need. TS gets away with the strict predicate
//! because it monomorphizes and never leaves a `void**` ArrayList in its type
//! table at all. MEASURED on this repro's two emits: TS has ZERO `void**`
//! fields (its ArrayLists are all monomorphized — `uint8_t*`, `size_t*`,
//! `__yo_struct_…_id_23*`), yo-self has EIGHT.
//! So the real divergence may be UPSTREAM: yo-self registers a
//! generic ArrayList in the codegen type table where TS registers only
//! monomorphized instances. Consider fixing THAT before, or instead of,
//! widening the predicate; at minimum land the two separately so the blast
//! radius stays attributable.
open(import("std/fmt"));
{ Mutex } :: import("std/sync/mutex");

main :: (fn() -> unit)({
  m := Mutex(i32).new(i32(5));
  println(`ok`);
});

export(main);
